[Music]  [Music]  with the upcoming release of breath the  wild sequel the legend of zelda series  is soon to be  20 games strong not counting spin-off  games remakes and  these specimens in order to keep a  series with this many games still going  strong to this day  nintendo had to innovate experiment and  change up the gameplay for each  iteration  as a result each game has their own  unique traits and mechanics which rarely  transition from one game to the next  some of these mechanics are solely  missed and while the desire for change  from the side of nintendo is completely  understandable  it would be nice if we could see some of  these traits return in future games  so for this video let's pretend for a  moment that we live in a perfect world  in which breathtak 2 will take  inspiration from every previous  installment  all the while still presenting us with  new stuff we didn't know we wanted as  nintendo has done so many  times in the past in that case which  aspect from each game could the zelda  team use to make brethwald's sequel the  perfect game  today i'm joined by xenogamer and  together we explore this question game  by game and in order of release date  and we each give our own take on a  specific one this video will cover the  first half of the total list so after  you're done watching this one be sure to  head over to zeno's channel and check  out part 2.  this one might seem a bit unnecessary  since breath the wild was already  largely based on the philosophy of the  first game  despite these inspirations however there  are still things about the original  which the sequel could take notes from  in particular the way it handled its  exploration and sense of discovery  despite adapting the non-linear style of  its nes elder the discoveries you made  in breath the wild were not in the same  vein as the original  the dungeons or should i say the divine  beasts were able to be tackled out of  order but they didn't need to be  discovered  since well they can literally be seen  stomping around or flying around from  afar  and secondly impa tells you exactly  where to find them  in its nes counterpart things were not  always as straightforward which made the  discovery of a dungeon  better sword secret shop or any other  mysteries all the more satisfying  now i am by no means saying that we need  to go back to bombing every wall and  burning every bush but the discoveries  you made in breath the wild definitely  lacked a certain variety  no matter what kind of secret area or  riddle you stumbled across  you knew that in the end the result was  going to be either a shrine or a korok  seed which in and of itself  kinda diminishes the surprise factor  secret caves were practically  non-existent in breath the wild  whenever you did find a bombable wall  don't expect anything more than just a  small little alcove with a treasure  chest or a korok seed  imagine climbing a hard-to-reach  mountain range and finding a small  little crevice and literally stumble  upon a full-fledged secret dungeon or  mini dungeon with its own unique style  and atmosphere that's the kind of stuff  the original definitely had going for it  [Music]  zelda 2 is a very different game by  zelda standards and  that's something i really personally  admire about it there's a reason this  game is called the black sheep of the  franchise  the game is more of an rpg than it is an  adventure game  battles take place outside of the  overworld map and you level up and gain  experience as you play  there are many unique things i could say  breath of the wild too should learn from  zelda 2  but above all i just wanted to embrace  one thing  be weird zelda 2 wasn't afraid to be  different  rpg and platforming elements a plethora  of new enemies  no ganon and even a freaking npc named  error  in my eyes zelda is at its best when it  embraces its less traditional side  goes off the rails a bit isn't afraid to  surprise players  breath of the wild has been endlessly  compared to the original legend of zelda  some might even go as far to say it's  basically a modern day remake  and i think that zelda 2 might have the  same kind of influence on breath of the  wild too  don't be afraid to stray from that line  in story or gameplay  dare to be different  a link to the past is the game which  first introduced the concept of shifting  between two different dimensions  in a sense doubling the size of the  overworld which in case of breath the  wall 2 couldn't be more fitting  anuma and his team have already  confirmed that the sequel will see us  revisiting the same hyrule scene in the  previous game  this has become somewhat of a cause for  concern amongst fans since in the three  and a half years since its release this  map has been turned inside out with  every nook and cranny visited and  discovered  so much so that some of us have resorted  to looking beyond the borders of what we  are allowed to see in the game  the light world dark world mechanics  seen in a link to the past was a great  way to turn an otherwise  quite simplistic overworld into  something much deeper  as opposed to twilight princess where  the other dimension was identical  just darker and more gloomy in a link to  the past the differences were pretty  significant  caves which were present in one were  absent in the other bridges broken or  intact and even entire areas were  completely different  such as the desert turning into a swamp  in the other world  something similar to this would make the  overworld in the sequel feel brand new  without actually adding new provinces  it remains to be seen if something like  this is doable since the map  is a lot bigger than the one in a link  to the past but maybe instead it could  be restricted to only certain areas  i played through link's awakening for  the first time last year with the  release of its switch remake  and i loved the game but was surprised  by what stood out to me the most as i  played through it  a mystery a mystery i already knew the  answer to  i played this game 27 years after its  initial release  so i had been spoiled on the events of  the story long before i booted up the  game  and from the very beginning i knew the  true nature of coho li island  but that still stuck with me as the most  essential part  to what makes this game so good the  mystery  and on top of that the overall  atmosphere  link's awakening tells a very simple  story but it's how that story unfolds  and affects you  that makes it so magical the way it  changes how you look at the world around  you  it takes everything you've grown to care  about and tells you if you beat the game  you're pulling the trigger on all that  and that's what breath of the wild 2  could learn from link's awakening the  way it uses atmosphere to drive the  game's storytelling  as well as how it pulls off an excellent  mystery  leading to a tremendous plot twist  the first thing that would come to mind  regarding ocarina of time would of  course be that which is represented by  its title  time travel however we have already  covered shifting between two dimensions  in a link to the past which is very  similar in a lot of ways and  having both traveling between dimensions  and time travel would perhaps be a  little bit too much  besides ocarina of time isn't the only  game to feature time travel  instead i would opt for breath the wild  to take inspiration from ocarina of  time's turning point  a lot of 3d zelda games have some sort  of a turning point in the story but none  are as prevalent as the one in ocarina  of time  the contrast between the child section  and the adult section was  immense and was a real game changer for  the story all of a sudden you transition  from a fragile kid who is cast into a  dangerous destiny into a full-fledged  badass hero  not only that but so many other things  start snowballing as soon as you reach  this point  ganondorf stole the triforce zelda is  forced to flee and the world has been  destroyed or taken over in your absence  breath of the wild lacked such a turning  point  technically it did have one but it took  place before the events of the actual  game and can only be viewed through  memories  the sequel has a chance to rectify this  mistake and have us experience moments  like these through  actual gameplay say for instance the  first few dungeons take place before  ganondorf wakes up  and after he does the world changes  radically and we are presented with new  challenges and objectives  [Music]  there are many reasons i call majora's  mask one of my favorite zelda games  but one of the biggest reasons is  because in many cases  it made me care about the side quests  more than i cared about the main story  there is an elegance to the side quests  in majora's mask  unlike any other game in the series  every quest happens simultaneously over  a period of three days  and as a result of this and the main  driving mechanic of time travel  throughout the game  all of the work you've done to improve  these people's lives is lost to time in  the end  it's a very dark and surreal experience  that i feel would be near impossible to  replicate again  but even if breath of the wild 2 can't  exactly carry over all of the concepts  that made majora's mask so special  i still think it has plenty to learn  from this game  you don't just remember majora's side  quest because of the unique  circumstances of the overarching system  of time in the game  you remember them because of the  characterization they showed ask most  zelda fans and i bet they will tell you  to this day  majora's mask is the most alive a world  has felt in the zelda series  almost every character is memorable with  their own motivations and daily schedule  they realistically react to the world  around them in the doom they are faced  with  not only that but their side quests have  a place in the world  in breath of the wild side quests are  often relegated to generic tasks given  from faceless npc  travelers these people are never really  given characteristics  they exist to give you a quest to reward  you with a reward that's not even worth  your time  and then disappear from your memory  forever and that's where breath of the  wild 2  needs to learn from the example of  majora unique characters in personality  and quest designed to fit into the world  that are fun to complete  and offer rewards that are actually  worth your time  despite being great games on their own  orkolf ages and seasons didn't introduce  too many new things aegis had time  travel and season sees you shifting  between different seasons which  influences the overworld both of which  we've seen in the past in some shape or  form  but things change when we look at the  games not as individual entities but as  a pair  by far the most unique aspect about them  was the ability to link the two  this allowed you to transfer certain  items from one to another  have characters traveling between two  worlds and getting the true ending to  each story requires combining the two  zelda games usually don't do alternative  or true endings  but guess which one does breath of the  wild and not only is it one of the few  games to have a true ending it's also  the first time we've seen a direct  sequel which takes place  almost right after and stars the same  link in zelda how cool would it be if  breathtwide 1 and 2 would somehow be  linked through save files  and it makes sense if you think about it  since if a player hasn't completed  certain quests in the original  they wouldn't know about it and thus it  would be strange if it pops up in the  sequel seemingly out of nowhere  as an example if you've never collected  the master sword in the original you  might start without it in the sequel and  you have to go get it first  or if you've never completed the  terrytown side quest the settlement  might not exist in breath the while too  not to mention all the amazing armor  sets and other collectibles we obtained  in breath of the wild which would be  nice if at least some of them would  transfer over to the new game  like the tunic of the wild for instance  [Music]  four swords might not be the most  beloved zelda game but it does exhibit  something i always loved seeing in the  zelda  series which is new villains four swords  was the first game to introduce us to  vati  the dark power hungry sorcerer as great  of a villain as ganondorf can be  there is yet to be an instance where i  have not enjoyed seeing a new character  step into the role as antagonist  whether it be secondary or main not only  is it nice to see a new villain we can  face off against every once in a while  but as commonplace as it's become to see  ganon or ganondorf playing the role of  series villain  seeing new villains in the series is  something i've come to see is quite  refreshing  i'd love to see a totally new or even  returning side villain in breath of the  wild too  to mix up the formula just as four  swords did  in many ways  this one is quite on the nose but yeah  sea exploration  it's safe to say that exploring the open  waters in breath of the wild  sucked and this is without taking the  ability to dive on the water into  account  the raft was slow and tedious and it  would be nice if we have something  better like a sailboat to traverse the  big blue and aside from an easier way to  get around the seas  what good would sea exploration be if  there's nothing to explore  expanding the sea borders a bit more and  having more accessible islands would  obviously have to come with it  in terms of side quests even tide island  was definitely a fan favorite so it  would make sense to add some more  islands with cool stuff on them  perhaps even with new races enemies  wildlife and maybe even a dungeon hiding  on one of them  and that concludes part 1 of this list  if you like this video be sure to head  over to xenogamer's channel and check  out part 2  where we go over the rest of the games  in the series a big thanks to zeno for  joining me in this video  and as always a massive thanks to my  patreon supporters who make these videos  possible  one of which has just decided to become  a member a big thanks to lucas should  play zelda for joining  and indeed lucas you should play zelda  what are you waiting for  next week you will once again be treated  to a collaboration  this time with none other than our  favorite scottish zelda channel  hyrulegamer i hope to see you then this  is dawn  signing off and have a good one

